- Hey Believe Nation, thank
you so much for watching.

00:06:37.267 --> 00:06:39.669
If you want to learn
more about this subject,

00:06:39.669 --> 00:06:41.171
get a little bit deeper on it

00:06:41.171 --> 00:06:42.946
I'm going to share some extra bonus clips

00:06:42.946 --> 00:06:44.332
that I hope you'll enjoy.

00:06:44.332 --> 00:06:46.452
- You think there were
no hurdles or obstacles

00:06:46.452 --> 00:06:48.322
that I had overcome but

00:06:48.322 --> 00:06:51.767
even I got to high school I wasn't ranked.

00:06:51.767 --> 00:06:53.874
I wasn't ranked, I wasn't highly touted

00:06:53.874 --> 00:06:55.451
as a high school prospect.

00:06:55.451 --> 00:06:57.977
I had nobody really
running, knocking on my door

00:06:57.977 --> 00:06:59.494
saying, "Please please please come play

00:06:59.494 --> 00:07:03.192
"for our school." until
Coach McKillip called.

00:07:03.192 --> 00:07:05.504
Everything happens for
a reason and there's

00:07:05.504 --> 00:07:07.127
a story to everything.

00:07:07.127 --> 00:07:09.596
And if you take time

00:07:09.596 --> 00:07:12.013
to realize what your dream is

00:07:13.919 --> 00:07:16.220
and what you really want in
life, no matter what it is

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:20.319
whether it's sports, whether
it's in other fields.

00:07:20.319 --> 00:07:22.586
You have to realize that
there's always work to do

00:07:22.586 --> 00:07:25.065
and you want to be the
hardest working person

00:07:25.065 --> 00:07:27.271
in whatever you do and you
put yourself in position

00:07:27.271 --> 00:07:28.650
to be successful.

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:31.111
And you have to have a
passion about what you do.

00:07:31.111 --> 00:07:32.549
And basketball was mine,

00:07:32.549 --> 00:07:34.314
and that's what's carried
me through this point.

00:07:34.314 --> 00:07:37.273
- The choice you have as an underdog is

00:07:37.273 --> 00:07:39.965
if you're going to choose to fight

00:07:39.965 --> 00:07:42.663
and choose to work to fight to win

00:07:42.663 --> 00:07:45.206
there are a series of
strategies available to you.

00:07:45.206 --> 00:07:47.323
But they are all more costly

00:07:47.323 --> 00:07:49.825
than the strategies
available to the favorite.

00:07:49.825 --> 00:07:53.325
So I give the example of a chapter about a

00:07:54.357 --> 00:07:57.488
a software mogul in Silicone
Valley, an Indian guy

00:07:57.488 --> 00:08:00.105
who coaches his daughters,
12-year-old daughter's

00:08:00.105 --> 00:08:01.438
basketball team.

00:08:02.466 --> 00:08:04.829
And they are, without talent.

00:08:04.829 --> 00:08:07.412
They are, by his own admission,

00:08:08.686 --> 00:08:11.570
this was like, they didn't
know how to play the game.

00:08:11.570 --> 00:08:14.337
He takes them all the way to
that National Championships.

00:08:14.337 --> 00:08:16.833
He does it by, he instructs them

00:08:16.833 --> 00:08:18.917
they're going to play
the full court press.

00:08:18.917 --> 00:08:20.762
Every minute of every game

00:08:20.762 --> 00:08:23.935
and defend every inch of the court, right?

00:08:23.935 --> 00:08:27.343
Now, that's actually a
very effective strategy

00:08:27.343 --> 00:08:30.426
particularly in age glass basketball.

00:08:31.568 --> 00:08:33.859
If you're the underdog
you're only chance at winning

00:08:33.859 --> 00:08:35.947
is to play a really
aggressive league defense.

00:08:35.947 --> 00:08:37.988
It requires, however

00:08:37.988 --> 00:08:42.327
that everyone in your
team expend maximum effort

00:08:42.327 --> 00:08:44.038
every minute of the game.

00:08:44.038 --> 00:08:47.239
You cannot loaf for an instant.

00:08:47.239 --> 00:08:49.003
You have to be in really good shape

00:08:49.003 --> 00:08:50.884
and you have to run yourself ragged.

00:08:50.884 --> 00:08:52.115
And you cannot let up.

00:08:52.115 --> 00:08:53.898
Most people will not play that way

00:08:53.898 --> 00:08:56.511
because it's too difficult.

00:08:56.511 --> 00:08:57.963
If I said to you we're
not going to be doing

00:08:57.963 --> 00:09:00.766
this elegantly designed plays

00:09:00.766 --> 00:09:03.062
and shooting gorgeous baskets

00:09:03.062 --> 00:09:04.764
and passing the ball.

00:09:04.764 --> 00:09:07.771
We're just going to be doing
this for the entire game.

00:09:07.771 --> 00:09:09.696
And you're going to be
exhausted at the end

00:09:09.696 --> 00:09:12.520
most kids would say, "That's
not why I signed up."

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.584
So it's like that's a kind
of clasic illustration of

00:09:15.584 --> 00:09:16.501
the effort,

00:09:17.867 --> 00:09:19.293
effort is the route

00:09:19.293 --> 00:09:21.458
one of the routes
available to the underdog.

00:09:21.458 --> 00:09:24.451
I cannot work you if, I may not be able

00:09:24.451 --> 00:09:26.920
to out spend you, but I can outwork you.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:28.503
Well, outworking is

00:09:30.086 --> 00:09:32.629
a tall order, it's not easy to do.

00:09:32.629 --> 00:09:37.236
That's as anyone who is
worked in a start up knows

00:09:37.236 --> 00:09:40.707
that's one of the stressful parts of it.

00:09:40.707 --> 00:09:42.724
But successful startups

00:09:42.724 --> 00:09:46.603
I've never seen any
actual data on this but

00:09:46.603 --> 00:09:48.716
do we think that people working for

00:09:48.716 --> 00:09:50.834
successful startups, work
longer hours than people

00:09:50.834 --> 00:09:53.273
who work for Fortune 500 companies?

00:09:53.273 --> 00:09:54.651
My guess is yes.

00:09:54.651 --> 00:09:57.602
- It wasn't until I started my own team

00:09:57.602 --> 00:10:00.262
when I realized that there are people

00:10:00.262 --> 00:10:04.148
with incredible talent, and no motivation.

00:10:04.148 --> 00:10:05.651
And then there are other
people that are willing

00:10:05.651 --> 00:10:07.292
to work their ass off

00:10:07.292 --> 00:10:09.723
who don't have a natural ability

00:10:09.723 --> 00:10:13.891
but will become successful
because they're so determined.

00:10:13.891 --> 00:10:14.724
And I saw,

00:10:16.437 --> 00:10:18.542
I saw it especially in a couple of guys

00:10:18.542 --> 00:10:20.845
that I first put on the Birdhouse team

00:10:20.845 --> 00:10:23.665
namely Andrew Reynolds, and Matt Beach.

00:10:23.665 --> 00:10:24.915
Matt Beach was,

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.538
in my eyes, the most naturally
talented skateboarder

00:10:28.538 --> 00:10:29.851
that I'd ever seen.

00:10:29.851 --> 00:10:32.017
Everything came easy in him

00:10:32.017 --> 00:10:34.744
and when I would request
these guys to go on tour

00:10:34.744 --> 00:10:36.954
all I ask of them is that they skate.

00:10:36.954 --> 00:10:38.187
For exhibitions, and what not.

00:10:38.187 --> 00:10:40.245
Matt wouldn't skate half the time.

00:10:40.245 --> 00:10:41.173
I flew him to Europe.

00:10:41.173 --> 00:10:42.988
I didn't even have any money then.

00:10:42.988 --> 00:10:45.104
In our company, but I was
flying these guys to Europe

00:10:45.104 --> 00:10:46.006
for competitions.

00:10:46.006 --> 00:10:48.059
He would go to the (mumbling)
and not feel like skating.

00:10:48.059 --> 00:10:50.732
And I was like, how can you do that?

00:10:50.732 --> 00:10:53.552
I was just thinking, not
just to me but to yourself.

00:10:53.552 --> 00:10:55.357
You have this incredible talent

00:10:55.357 --> 00:10:57.116
and you just don't care.

00:10:57.116 --> 00:10:59.164
And then I saw Andrew
Reynolds who was this

00:10:59.164 --> 00:11:02.061
scrappy little guy taking not very clean

00:11:02.061 --> 00:11:04.149
but, in terms of his style.

00:11:04.149 --> 00:11:05.646
But trying some of the hardest stuff,

00:11:05.646 --> 00:11:06.989
taking the heaviest hits

00:11:06.989 --> 00:11:08.897
but always skating his (bleeped) off.

00:11:08.897 --> 00:11:09.996
And always trying.

00:11:09.996 --> 00:11:12.446
And he has become probably
the most well-respected

00:11:12.446 --> 00:11:14.066
street skater today.

00:11:14.066 --> 00:11:16.262
Andrew Reynolds, I mean,
he formed Bakerbrand,

00:11:16.262 --> 00:11:18.095
Deathwish Skateboards.

00:11:18.976 --> 00:11:22.213
The community of the most
hardcore skaters these days

00:11:22.213 --> 00:11:23.713
call him The Boss.

00:11:24.603 --> 00:11:26.915
Because he developed his style because

00:11:26.915 --> 00:11:28.678
he worked his ass off.

00:11:28.678 --> 00:11:31.858
And he's the shining example of hard work.

